#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120641} :activity/id #uuid "0ef52fb7-e1f4-5106-bb36-6b94715ae636" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048616 :topic/id #uuid "aef82e82-0717-5dbd-b71e-34fa8441f954" :topic/math-academy-id 1795} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121148 :tutorial/content "The **principal normal vector** to a curve $C$ at a point $P$ is a unit vector that \n\n- is perpendicular to the unit tangent vector, and\n\n- points in the direction that the curve is bending.\n\nIn general, if the unit tangent vector is given by $\\mathbf T(t),$ then the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|},\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that $\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|\\neq 0.$\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that the curve $C$ is parametrized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\cos t, \\: 2\\sin t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0, \\pi].\n$$\n\nWe will compute the principal normal vector at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\nFirst, using the formula for the unit tangent, $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf f'(t)}{||\\mathbf f'(t)||},$ we find that the general unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)= \\left\\langle {-\\sin t}, \\cos t\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNow, when $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we have the point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ The unit tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and the principal normal vector $\\mathbf{N}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ at this point are shown below:\n\n![](images/d0/d0a30a44bb825a8a12ca54153b6223b9bf0c2b754245b21650a5664db5e1647e.png)\n\nTo calculate the principal normal vector, we start by calculating the derivative of the unit tangent function. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general principal normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{||{T}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $\\mathbf N\\left(\\dfrac\\pi 4\\right),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, it's easy to check that $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and $\\mathbf N'(t)$ are perpendicular for all $t$ by computing the dot product and showing that we get zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\cdot N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "03709847-4a63-5975-b20a-1d6693ceadb3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9376 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c3443d00-c515-5a63-93a0-747d76d2271c" :step/math-academy-id 626149 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119785 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119532 :question/id #uuid "8cb590c5-586d-56a1-af70-d693ea055419" :question/math-academy-id "e-14996"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050685}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442613}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"} :step/id #uuid "c9c3f801-f35a-5089-8d66-01d0fd9903d2" :step/math-academy-id 626150 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120643}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119533 :question/id #uuid "2f92f568-0bf5-58bd-87ce-e6be8d05fe69" :question/math-academy-id "e-14997"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484275}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"} :step/id #uuid "812571c6-b194-5ab1-b16a-bcf2ac6ffbe9" :step/math-academy-id 626153 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121149 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf T(t)$ be the unit tangent vector to some differentiable vector-valued function. Since $||\\mathbf T(t)|| = 1,$ and $\\mathbf T(t)$ is obviously parallel to itself, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) \\cdot \\mathbf T(t) = 1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above relationship with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T'(t) \\cdot \\mathbf T(t) + \\mathbf T(t) \\cdot \\mathbf T'(t) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t)\\cdot T(t)+{T}^{′}(t)\\cdot T(t) & =0 \\\\ 2{T}^{′}(t)\\cdot T(t) & =0 \\\\ {T}^{′}(t)\\cdot T(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, provided that $\\mathbf T'(t)\\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then it must be the case that $\\mathbf T'(t)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathbf T(t).$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf T'(t)$ measures the rate of change of $\\mathbf T(t).$ However, since $\\mathbf T(t)$ is a unit vector for all $t,$ it changes only in its direction. The vector $\\mathbf T'(t)$ measures this rate of change in direction. \n\nTo form the principal normal $\\mathbf N(t),$ we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t),$ which leads to the definition\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{||\\mathbf T'(t)||}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bd9190b9-f59c-5c06-987a-c6269ec9b3a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9377 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Principal Normal"} :step/id #uuid "ee849a7a-e449-5317-872e-183c46fabb72" :step/math-academy-id 626156 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119534 :question/id #uuid "f5e6498b-73f9-5760-81bb-14e9acc495f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14998"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442612}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"} :step/id #uuid "8e583ccb-952c-52c6-b8a9-e4b716f0d3c4" :step/math-academy-id 626157 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121150 :tutorial/content "Consider the straight line given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t\\,[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf we attempt to calculate its principal normal vector, we will get an interesting result. First, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\begin{aligned}-2+5t \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\,[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the case of a straight line, $\\mathbf T(t)$ is simply the (normalized) direction vector, as we would expect. \n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\,[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}]=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{T}'(t)$ is the zero vector, we can't normalize it as usual. If we try to apply the usual formula we get an undefined result due to division by zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{0}{‖0‖}=\\frac{0}{0}=\\text{undefined}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a straight line does not have a principal normal.\n\nThe intuition behind this result is that the principal normal points in the direction in which a curve is bending, but a straight line does not bend at all." :tutorial/id #uuid "5c27df4b-0d17-5407-997b-c240d492eeca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9378 :tutorial/title "Lines Do Not Have Principal Normal Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "1aa2d247-5dd4-5fdf-a44a-9cf0a8cd1e0d" :step/math-academy-id 626160}] :activity/title "Principal Normal Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120013}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484329} :activity/id #uuid "de0bb7f6-cebe-5043-a82b-316bfc3bd5eb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048616 :topic/id #uuid "aef82e82-0717-5dbd-b71e-34fa8441f954" :topic/math-academy-id 1795} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a35ec37-2a33-551d-8e81-252c71bec628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483673} :answer-field/id #uuid "83efe503-c6c7-5398-8b5a-e3c31636860c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "34386db1-c26a-52c0-8482-81ad51998468" :question/math-academy-id "q-358781" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{5}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-4\\,k).\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{5}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-4\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)}{\\frac{3}{5}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3dc5246d-6b1c-5f6e-8d4b-2f726b761e7e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61b2cbe-e283-5d14-b24c-72dded057638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+\\,j+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398328} {:answer/id #uuid "843c6fdf-9f38-56ac-8525-881bcff219b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(2\\,i-\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398329} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b83354-a308-50ea-9ee4-85aaf20726b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398330} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1c6878-8ab0-51e2-8005-62b32908bc7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(-2\\,i+\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398331} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7cd7b0-03c7-513f-a03d-32eb59e6232b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i-2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398332}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398330} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a28c00-4228-59a1-bd55-b03951c2d0fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0d57e43b-681d-5aa1-a53c-acdd85847d41" :question/math-academy-id "q-127600" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=-2\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2(0)}{({0}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =0(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)+\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-j+k}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-j\\cdot j+k\\cdot k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-1+1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "51cd3660-dac6-5f65-9a77-3570d44fead1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484335}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "375ef518-4d6a-56b5-8701-cd9802686411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342695} {:answer/id #uuid "41e5f4d8-856b-52f5-85b3-07ac865cef2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342784} {:answer/id #uuid "69d5fca1-eec8-5d54-b1fc-d0f9751e2efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343141} {:answer/id #uuid "745b8d3b-d175-5c42-a346-9db9b829c2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343245} {:answer/id #uuid "af1ce442-73ac-5780-9670-756b226a0a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343779} :answer-field/id #uuid "74402250-c07b-5643-a659-e21ce9c35c06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b15f4c73-c376-5fbd-b671-915cb2fd0f2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75491" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨3,0,-4⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{5}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7532dca0-e492-58d7-9fd4-a9d9dfcd23c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484320}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c0bb344-e2e7-53b7-a86b-a70876c813ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483884}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483884} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d3585e-134e-53e5-b3ff-d54d2085a716" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd425a39-318f-5566-a2e2-b27e6f8eab87" :question/math-academy-id "q-223098" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩.\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$⟨$\n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =2‖⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩‖ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "97701ad9-732a-5b50-b89b-ace9fb233738" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484353}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d617821-6685-519e-9a4d-28281ac958a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288333} {:answer/id #uuid "099d7c1b-7772-5cc6-9777-a7e269acac3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sin(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483587}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483587} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ee93b5a-7a7f-5de6-b120-52987f2cfbac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "705b4f99-555b-54e9-8531-2285040b4156" :question/math-academy-id "q-223101" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,k).\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$k$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k)}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d0a76b8b-38c9-52ec-a2f0-64392234672c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484317}}] :activity/title "Principal Normal Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483564}]}
